In `PUT_RemoveContract`, you see that I created a contract where I observed certain changes in the ArrayList. I also made assert that test the properties of the ArrayList after the remove operation.

This should allow the testing of remove functionality.

Remember to update your PexSettings to use your test class, like this example:

```csharp
<PexMethodUnderTest>PUT_RemoveContract</PexMethodUnderTest>
```

Then you just need to run your Pex test. It will generate inputs and mutate them to find failures or test if your remove contract works. 


Here is how it looks when you run your Pex test:
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void TestMethod1()
{
    var testClass = new ArrayListContractTest();

    // The assumption here 
    var arrList = new ArrayList {1, 2, 3, 2, 1 };

    // runs Pex 
    testClass.PUT_RemoveContract(arrList, 2);
}
```

This `TestMethod1` will start Pex and the mutations will find the problem and fail the test. This guarantees that your contract for remove is correct.



You can find more information about ArrayList testing with Microsoft Pex [here](https://channel9.msdn.com/Series/Pex-for-Visual-Studio-2011-Concurrency-and-Parallelism/Pex-for-Visual-Studio-2011-Parallelism-Lesson-2).

