  Condition: $old_arrList_Count == 0 
  Condition: $old_arrList_Count >= $new_arrList_Count 
  Condition: $new_arrList_Count == 0 
[Rejected] 
  [x86-32]    Test(ArrayListContractTest, PUT_RemoveContract) 
  [Reason]    
    Condition: $new_arrList_Count == 0 
  [Output]   
  Condition: $old_arrList_Count == 0 
  Condition: $old_arrList_Count >= $new_arrList_Count 
  Condition: $new_arrList_Count == 0 


In this example, when the test runner rejects the test suite because the condition $old_arrList_Count >= $new_arrList_Count for PUT_RemoveContract fails, it is actually because PexObserve.ValueForViewing calls are failing. 
Any thoughts?

A: The issue is because of your assertion. Assert needs conditions to hold true while testing. In your debug assert, it checks that new array list count should be less than or equal to old count. But it will always fail because as you are deleting elements, count of ArrayList will keep on increasing. 
Instead, what you should assert and check are whether removing an element decreases the size of the data structure by 1. Because each remove operation decreases count of elements at least by one. 
Also, you can see that PexObserve.ValueForViewing is making all these checks separately, so assert conditions need to be fine-grained to keep all properties intact.
You have to modify your assertion to something like this:
Debug.Assert((Old_arrListCount > 0 && New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount - 1) || 
               (Old_arrListCount == 0 && New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount));

The assertion now checks whether the original list (before removing) had some elements and after removing one, new list has one less count (or if original list was empty, new list is also empty).
Alternative, use PexAssert.IsTrue:
    PexAssert.IsTrue((arrList.Count == Old_arrListCount - 1) || 
                     ((arrList.Count == 0) && (Old_arrListCount == 0)));

This will assert that (after remove) count of array list is one less than old count or if both--------------------
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListCount <= Old_arrListCount);


	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ new array does not include x

            Assert.IsFalse(New_arrListContainsX);


	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ if x was not in the array list, both indexOf and lastIndexOf return -1

            Assert.AreEqual(-1, New_arrListIndexOfX);
            Assert.AreEqual(-1, New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
        }
    }
}

