I am not sure what is happening here. How can I resolve this? Is this because my code is not correctly structured or something else is wrong?
UPDATE:
I am using PexContracts for contract testing and as per documentation, I need to have at least one assume under my tests. In this case the function should have at least one input that satisfies the assumed precondition. But it is showing that there is not any inputs that satisfy the assume. Can you please guide as to what should I do in this case.

A: Your function isn't meeting the assume under constraints on its parameters. If you'd look it closely, you'll notice that the parameter 'x' cannot be in range [0, Count] for ArrayList. 

Because of this, all inputs for the Remove operation will always either not be in the list or will be out of bounds, and thus, you won't discover any inputs that will satisfy the Assume pre-condition.

The solution is to change the assume under constraints so that the parameter 'x' can be in any int range. 

I hope this helps.

A: The problem is, according to Pex, there's no input combination that satisfies the assume precondition.

The reason is that ArrayList.Remove method requires an item to be present in the list to remove. Your current contract states that 'x' should be in the list to pass the assume condition, but since your input ArrayList can have any values, there's certainly no combination that fulfills this requirement.

To fix this issue you would need to either change your input ArrayList elements so an element exists in the collection before the call to Remove, in which case you could use PexAssume that the condition is satisfied, or make your contract more flexible so that 'x' can be any integer or even null, and then when you specify the assumptions for your test you would set the condition that 'x' should exist in the ArrayList.

I hope this clarifies things.

A: The error you are getting is because the Pex testing system is unable to find any inputs that satisfy the assume precondition. The reason is that the test is trying to remove an element from the ArrayList where that element may not exist. 
Please remember that the AssumePrecondition is useful in ensuring that the test is working with certain conditions for input parameters. This condition has nothing to do with the actual behavior of your application's code. So, in your case, you are trying to force a Remove operation on an ArrayList where the element is not present, leading to an error. 
A better way of achieving contract testing for ArrayList's Remove method would be not specifying any assume conditions for the test. The Pex testing system would then generate inputs automatically and test the behavior of the Remove method. It might give you different results, but you would be able to see the behavior of the actual code.
Try this modification:
public partial class ArrayListContractTest
{
    [PexMethod]
    public void CustomTest(ArrayList arrList)
    {
        int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;

        // Assign an element to remove, not mandatory that it should exist in the list.
        int elementToRemove = 0; 

        if(Old_arrListCount > 0)
        {
            arrList.Remove(elementToRemove);
        }

        int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;

        // Assertions
        Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListCount <= Old_arrListCount);
    }
}

In this test, we're assigning 'elementToRemove', which can take any possible value--------------------